course: dictionary
lesson: 5323
title: Vocabulary in context: kerb
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A word like kerb can be easy to picture once you read the words around it. «When it rains, you stand back from the kerb, because passing cars splash water from the puddles at the edge of the road.» Stand back, splash, puddles and the edge of the road all point to one place: where the pavement meets the road.
explain: Now test your idea by swapping in a meaning. «Her guide dog stops dead at the kerb so she knows a road is there.» If kerb means the edge of the pavement, the dog stops at the edge, and the woman knows the road starts there. The meaning fits, so you can trust it.
explain: Kerb is British spelling. In American English the same word is spelled curb, so if you read an American book, look out for curb. It means the same thing.
fact: A kerb is the raised edge of a pavement where it meets the road.
fact: In American English, the same word is spelled curb.
fact: A dropped kerb is a low kerb, which wheelchair users need in order to cross the road.
fact: The Green Cross Code says: stop at the kerb, look and listen both ways, and cross when it is clear.
fact: Never step off the kerb without looking first.
exercise: «When it rains, you stand back from the kerb.» What is a kerb? | A puddle in the middle of the road | *The raised edge where the pavement meets the road | A bench in the park | A type of car
exercise: What does the Green Cross Code say to do at the kerb? | Step off without looking | *Stop, look and listen both ways, then cross when it is clear | Wait for a car to park on it | Run across the road quickly
exercise: In American English, how is kerb spelled? | kern | kerb | *curb | cerb
test: «Her guide dog stops dead at the kerb so she knows a road is there.» Where does the dog stop? | *The edge where the pavement meets the road | The shops | The middle of the road | A puddle
test: What is a dropped kerb for? | To mark the start of a shop | To stop puddles forming | To make the road wider | *To help wheelchair users cross more easily
exam: «Bike lanes separated by kerbs keep cars out.» What do the kerbs keep out of the bike lane? | Rain | Pedestrians | Bikes | *Cars
